│     The fix preserves the relative ratio of left to right padding when       │
│ truncation is necessary.                                                     │
│     The bug was caused by the `Padding` class in `rich/padding.py` not       │
│ correctly handling cases where the requested padding exceeded the maximum    │
│ console width when `expand=True` or when the total width was capped. This    │
│ resulted in a negative `content_width` which was capped at 0, but the        │
│ original (excessive) padding values were still used, causing the total       │
│ rendered width to exceed the limit.                                          │
│                                                                              │
│     I fixed this by:                                                         │
│     1.  Calculating the `content_width` first.                               │
│     2.  Checking if `content_width` is negative (meaning padding exceeds the │
│ available width).                                                            │
│     3.  If it is negative, I set `content_width` to 0 and recalculated       │
│ `left_pad` and `right_pad` proportionally to the available width, ensuring   │
│ their sum equals the maximum width.                                          │
│     4.  Updating the rendering logic to use these recalculated `left_pad`    │
│ and `right_pad` values instead of the original `self.left` and `self.right`. │
│                                                                              │
│     This ensures                                                             │
